JSON-safe, fail-closed evidence records for the SMRF design review.

These records describe checks within an explicitly declared research scope;
they are not a building-code certification. Missing evidence is not success.

Evidence repairs of the code-check audit of the genetic search (2026-10-30): findings F03-F08.

  F03  slab credit: a selected layout is credited as developed only where the slab record's own development
       check passes on the current floor context; the exterior perimeter-hook treatment is kept;
  F04  frame-beam axial applicability (ACI 318-19 18.6.4.7) from the signed envelope of the solved actions;
  F05  splice placement by bar grade (18.2.7.2: Type 2 anywhere only for Grade 60);
  F06  saved drift checks rebuilt from their numbers instead of trusted;
  F07  grouped hoop identity: missing selection, selected mismatch and selected match kept apart;
  F08  the qualification scope follows the system actually designed.

Rows and records are small and written by hand; the fixture frame is not a designed structure.

Severity plus demand-sized preference of the genetic search (selection policy severity_preferred_jumps_v1,
2026-11-01).

  P1  a severe failure with useful screened proposals gets the configured preferred allocation of the guided draws:
      the branch frequency is the declared share, and the probabilities normalize;
  P2  on the numbers of o0002 of the 10-minute jumps run, the strongly improving 22 x 36 in beam is far more likely than
      the small 5 -> 6 ksi step, and than the 8 ksi partial; an improvement is always the diagnostic's own unit's
      estimate against the same unit's baseline (a family grade option is governed by another band);
  P3  a marginal failure does not trigger the preference; drift and column checks get no beam estimate;
  P4  a screened proposal keeps its preference when a local step also proposes it; repeated evidence, both groups of a
      tie and two operators add no priority;
  P5  no useful jump, invalid or stale evidence, a negligible or no improvement and an exhausted pool fall back
      explicitly to the normal branch; an estimate that needs a cage that does not thread the frozen column cages is no
      beam-only reach of the target; a threading-constrained failure keeps its column alternatives and says the
      beam-only target is unmet;
  P6  seeded draws repeat and every logged probability is the exact distribution's;
  P8  a unit's own smallest target option: the screen gives it per unit (a family's shortlist follows its worst unit;
      confirmed only when that unit was priced at every smaller option), the route annotates its existing proposal of
      it without adding one (every policy draws over the same genomes), also when the gene has no shortlist or the
      shortlist is another lever's move, with the option's own gene-level fields, and the pool prefers it over the
      family's larger option; the drawn annotations and their routes are counted apart; the entry logs the unit's
      estimate pool beside the gene's; an excluded proposal is one row per key and reason, a partly eligible genome
      keeps the rows of its other keys, and eligible entries and already-drawn rows carry every diagnostic and lever;
      the note names column proposals only where the failure has them;
  P7  the controller: the preferred branch's children are evaluated and assessed like any other and never accepted by
      estimate, a pool candidate drawn for a parent (either branch) is not drawn from the preferred branch again, the
      logged distribution is complete and normalized, the counters keep proposed, drawn, completed, errored and
      accepted apart, the policy and its parameters bind the basis (a change refuses the resume), and the configuration
      and runner declare it.

Synthetic diagnostics and screen summaries on the fixture frame, no analysis: nothing here is a designed or accepted
structure.

The floor path under a grouped design: by-line floor models, one transfer per distinct floor (2026-10-02).

* a floor described line by line with equal lines solves to the one-beam floor's transfer;
* unlike lines change the load path, keep force and first-moment equilibrium, and are graded to their
  own beam faces with the same number of cells in every bay;
* floors are matched by the signature of their mechanical inputs (two distinct floors in the fixture);
* the frame receives each floor's own transfer, and the base reaction is the hand total;
* a uniform transfer, a transfer of another design, an edited transfer and a missing floor are refused.

Beam lanes from the joint's bar inventory at a column transition (2026-10-31; since the review of the same day,
joint_inventory_lanes_v2__swept_bend_intervals: each moved bar blocks its whole bend interval, test_cage_review_corrections).

  * at every joint without a moved bar the lanes are the core cage's, exactly, and the bar-row basis keeps its form;
  * a 2 in face step bends the column bars through the lanes: a 32 in core of twenty #10 passes three #9 per layer on
    its own and none through the joint's inventory, while a 1 in step keeps all three;
  * the bar rows, the selection evidence and the authoritative threading row (beam.bars_thread_column) read the same
    lanes, and the bar-row basis records the rule where it lowers a lane;
  * the column chain counts and cuts the cage pairs whose band-boundary joint closes the beam lanes, and the chain it
    selects from a pool of threading cages keeps them open.

Synthetic demands on the fixture frame; geometry and selection only.

The perimeter hook of the slab mats at two datums and its location factor (2026-11-01).

  P  psi_o by ACI 318-19 Table 25.4.3.2 on the declared corner convention: the fixture's corners (26 in columns,
     18 in beams) are inside the column core with covers of 6 in (psi_o 1.0); narrow 16 in columns leave covers of
     2 in, so psi_o is 1.25 and 25.4.3.4 asks for ties the mats do not have; an end at an edge column keeps 1.0;
     missing geometry raises; the #6 at 3 in hook at 5 ksi is 18.70 in at psi_o 1.25 against 16.0 in (the row
     fails, the screen rejects that spacing) and 14.96 in at 1.0; a corner needing ties leaves no layout;
  S/D2  an end that develops at the support face but not at the joint face (16 in column, 18 in perimeter beam,
     #6 at 4 in: 15.53 in against 16.0 and 15.0 in) is undeveloped for sizing, developed for the strong-column rule,
     and the hinges keep the support face; D4: 18.6.3.2 reads the larger negative at the face;
  I  per end and sign: sizing <= hinge <= strong-column <= composite, and Mpr >= the strong-column value; on a
     layout developed at the support face everywhere the strong-column values equal the hinge values;
  K  a sentinel in the strong-column keys changes nothing in sizing, the hinge properties or the saved end
     strengths; the strength cache key follows each exterior end's column;
  U  the uniform route's context rebuild reproduces the corner hooks and flags a changed column.

The fixture frame is not a designed structure. No solve.

The procedure-based slab evidence path, P1 (Design.SMRF_Slab_Procedure; profile schema v2), 2026-11-01.

  L  loading: a v2 example loads inactive_unreviewed and asserts nothing; a changed method file makes it
     inactive_method_changed; a v2 profile keeps the domain instead of the floor list and the single thickness, and
     declares its plan; a reviewed procedure refuses TEST FIXTURE and PROBE text; an asserted flag whose dispositions
     are not all accepted is refused; the v1 example keeps its identity;
  A  apply: the procedure is carried under every status, the assertions only under an active one;
  D  the domain: a floor's beams, grade and supports, and the slab thickness on the ladder; malformed input is outside;
  I  instance evidence: the load-case inventory recomputed from the slab's own loads; the checks pass on complete
     evidence and fail on a missing piece; masking keeps the numbers; an edited record fails the replay;
  S  the slab stage: the scope fails closed under an inactive procedure and outside the domain; a plan that does not
     resolve on a floor is recorded as that floor's unverified evidence, not raised; each floor's instance is kept and
     the flags of a floor that fails are masked; the uniform route refuses a procedure.

No solve: the slab builders are patched.

Structural slab gates and the declared fire-resistance exclusion (2026-11-01).

  P  producer: the two-way shear path and the slab-column local steel (ACI 318-19 8.6.1.2) are each resolved on
     their own: a pass only with the beam-supported premise and that item's own assertion, otherwise an unknown row
     with its own reason code (never a non-blocking review); the fire row is a declared scope exclusion;
  E  slab-strength eligibility: an unresolved gate is an unknown check, the exclusion is listed apart;
  C  contract: both gates are required completion rows, each blocks acceptance on its own, a known failure beside
     an unresolved gate classifies as unresolved, not as a check failure; the fire row is skipped only as the
     declared exclusion and is invalid as a pass, fail or review;
  G  the search's normalized checks skip the excluded row;
  Q  qualification: no verification flag passes the excluded item or the local-steel gate, the exclusion is listed
     apart, and a passing fire row blocks;
  A  assertions: the local-steel flag lives on SlabActionAssertions, outside all_asserted; a profile that predates
     it loads with its identity unchanged, and a non-boolean value is refused.

No solve.
